{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790458309,"nonce":"WGTnsSPAtoaDO1XlaZkTDcXiKdI=","message":"Distance to caribou_gpu under wasm AOT (survey, 2026-09-26). What works: caribou build --target wasm32-wasip1 links a Haxe program against caribou's runtime object and runs it on ash-wasm-run.\nGaps, in dependency order:\n1. caribou natives in an AOT program. Ash's AOT refuses a host native that carries a context word (crates/ash/src/llvm/function/natives.rs: 'cannot be linked ahead of time'), and every caribou native is one (a slot per native). In a build where the natives are not registered, they become HDLL slots that hlp_aot_native fills at start from a library 'caribou' that doesn't exist, so they raise when reached. Needed: a host resolver seam that answers (fn, context) for lib 'caribou', and AOT call sites that pass the context; or, for typed members, the static link of 0683393. A plugin's classes are C functions with C signatures, so they can link directly by the same rule.\n2. Plugins on wasm. caribou-plugin loads with libloading. On wasm a plugin is an Ash side module (dylink.0, PIC, -Zbuild-std; ash docs/wasm/hdlls.md), opened through env.ash_host_dlopen/dlsym as WrenLift's native libraries now are.\n3. The GPU backend. cb_gpu's wasm build uses wgpu's webgpu backend, which rides wasm-bindgen and web-sys: wasm32-unknown-unknown with JS glue, which Ash's wasip1 pipeline and linker don't process. So on this target the plugin's browser backend calls WebGPU through host imports whose JS side is generated from WebIDL. That is ead67cc (interface operations, callbacks, unions) extended to emit the imports and the shim: a handle table for objects, dictionaries marshalled by the records caribou-bindgen already generates, strings as ptr/len, Promises as suspension points. Only the subset gpu.api.rs uses.\n4. Promises. requestAdapter, requestDevice and mapAsync suspend the calling fiber. Ash's browser host suspends with its own fiber transform (ASH_WASM_FIBERS), not JSPI, so the shim resolves a Promise by resuming the fiber through caribou's scheduler.\n5. The harness. Ash's browser host plus the generated shim; a canvas instead of the window plugin's surface.\n6. The core on wasm, as it bears on this: e964da6 (implicit rooting sites, needing a stress run) and 7729de0 (dynamic calls into Haxe through Ash's trampolines, needed if a GPU callback calls a Haxe closure).","files":null}]}